{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Raymond Berenger and a ruler of the County of Tripoli. He was born around 1150 AD and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III was a member of the nobility and played an important role in the history of the County of Tripoli.\n\n* **Early Life and Reign** *\nRaymond III became the Count of Tripoli at a young age following the death of his father in 1152 AD. He was succeeded by his mother Hodierna of Jerusalem who acted as regent until he came of age. During his early years as count Raymond III had to deal with the threat of the Zengid dynasty and the Muslim forces that had conquered much of the Levant. He formed alliances with other Christian rulers and nobles in the region in order to protect his county from the Muslim threat.\n\nRaymond III also had to deal with the internal politics of the County of Tripoli. He had to balance the power of the nobles and the church in order to maintain control and stability in the county. He also had to deal with the issue of succession as he had no clear heir to the throne.\n\n* **Marriage and Children** *\nRaymond III married Isabella of Jerusalem in 1163 AD. The marriage was a strategic one as it united the County of Tripoli with the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Isabella was a member of the royal family of Jerusalem and the marriage helped to strengthen the ties between the two kingdoms. Raymond III and Isabella had several children including Raymond IV Count of Tripoli who succeeded his father as count.\n\n* **Later Life and Death** *\nRaymond III died in 1187 AD during the Third Crusade. He was a key player in the early years of the Crusade and played an important role in the defense of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He died in the siege of Acre and was succeeded by his son Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. The County of Tripoli c